BCC_Q({"y": 2019, "n": 9, "part": "B", "topic": "algo", "title": "Making Stitches", "answer": 3, "story": "<p>A sewing machine can make four different types of stitches. The rules\nthat the machine follows are shown.<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p><img alt=\"A description of the diagram follows.\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:60.0%\"/><\/p>\n<\/div>\n<button onclick=\"hideSeek('longdesc6')\" style=\"box-shadow:none\">Hide/Reveal Description Diagram for Making Stitches<\/button>\n<div id=\"longdesc6\" style=\"display:none\">\n<p>Three circles in a row connected by arrows as follows:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>A thick blue arrow on the left side points to the first circle.<\/li>\n<li>An arrow labelled with a diagonal down stitch points from the first circle to the second circle.<\/li>\n<li> An arrow labelled with an upside down capital L stitch points from the second circle to the third circle.<\/li>\n<li>An arrow with a capital L stitch points from the third circle to the second circle.<\/li>\n<li>An arrow with a diagonal up stitch points from the second circle to the first circle.<\/li>\n<li>An outlined arrow on the right side points away from the third circle.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<hr class=\"longdesc\"/>\n<\/div>\n<p>The machine starts a new line of stitches by following the thick blue\narrow on the left.<\/p>\n<p>Then the machine moves from circle to circle following in the\ndirection of the arrows. Every time an arrow is followed, the machine\nmakes the stitch shown on that arrow. If a circle has more than one\narrow leading out of it, the user of the machine can choose to follow\neither one of the arrows.<\/p>\n<p>The machine finishes by following the outlined arrow on the\nright.<\/p>", "question": "<p>Which line of stitches <span><strong>cannot<\/strong><\/span> be made\nusing the above rules?<\/p>\n<ol class=\"mc\">\n<li><span><img alt=\"The following stitches, from left to right: diagonal down, upside down L, L, upside down L, L, upside down L, L, upside down L.\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:50px\"/><\/span><\/li>\n<li><span><img alt=\"The following stitches from left to right: diagonal down, upside down L, L, upside down L, L, diagonal up, diagonal down, diagonal up, diagonal down, upside down L.\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:50px\"/><\/span><\/li>\n<li><span><img alt=\"The following stitches from left to right: diagonal down, upside down L, L, diagonal up, diagonal down, upside down L, L, upside down L.\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:50px\"/><\/span><\/li>\n<li><span><img alt=\"The following stitches from left to right: diagonal down, diagonal up, diagonal down, upside down L, L, upside down L, diagonal down, diagonal up, diagonal down, upside down L.\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:50px\"/><\/span><\/li>\n<\/ol>", "answerHtml": "<p>(D) <span><img alt=\"The following stitches are made: diagonal down, diagonal up, diagonal down, upside down L, L, upside down L, diagonal down, diagonal up, diagonal down, upside down L.\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:50px\"/><\/span><\/p>", "explanation": "<p>The stitch in Option D <span><strong>cannot<\/strong><\/span> be\nmade.<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p><img alt=\"The location of an upside down L stitch followed by a diagonal down stich is highlighted in the line of stiches from option D.\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:100px\"/><\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>The problem (shown above) is that a <span><img alt=\"upside down L\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:50px\"/><\/span> stitch is followed by a <span><img alt=\"diagonal down\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:50px\"/><\/span> stitch. This is not possible because after a\n<span><img alt=\"upside down L\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:50px\"/><\/span> stitch, the machine can only\ncreate a <span><img alt=\"L\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:50px\"/><\/span> stitch or finish the line of\nstitches.<\/p>\n<p>The stitches in Options A, B, and C can all be made. We can see this\nby following arrows in the rules stitch by stitch. For example, Option A\ncan be made by following arrows in the directions right, right, left,\nright, left, right, left, right.<\/p>", "connections": "<p>The diagram presented in this problem is a <em>state diagram<\/em> of\na <em>finite-state machine<\/em>. Finite state machines are used in\ncomputer science to describe systems which can be in one of a finite set\nof possible states at any given time and move from one state to another\nby means of a finite set of actions. The next state of the system\ndepends only on the current state and the executed action. Usually, the\n<em>initial state<\/em> and the <em>final states<\/em> of the system are\nalso specified.<\/p>\n<p>More specifically, this problem is also a nice example of the\n<em>syntax validation process<\/em>. Before searching for logical errors\nin computer programs (this search is called <em>debugging<\/em>), the\n<em>syntax<\/em> of the program is checked. That is, a <em>compiler<\/em>\nor <em>interpreter<\/em> first has to check that the text of the program\nis a valid sequence of characters much like in this problem where we are\nchecking whether or not a particular line of stitches could have been\nmade using the given rules.<\/p>", "mc": 1});